---
/*
  CaptureDialog — the ✚ and the box behind it (docs/plans/archive/14-capture.md §4,
  10-hq.md §10c).

  ONE DOOR, EVERY ROOM. Mounted from AdminLayout rather than from a page, so
  the thought can be dumped from wherever you happen to be standing. That is
  10-hq §10c as written: *"a global control (the ✚, bottom-right) reachable
  from every room, plus its own room for the pile."*

  ⚠ AND IT IS DELIBERATELY NOT A CARD ON TODAY, which is what 14 §4 originally
  said. The two plans disagreed and §10c is the newer thought, decided after
  six rounds on the Today lab: *"the daily summary and the dumping ground are
  two separate concerns."* Today answers *what is my day*; a dump box on it
  would be a zone that answers nothing about today, sitting among five that do.

  A <dialog>, so you keep your place. The alternative was navigating to the
  Notes room to type — which costs you whatever room you were in, and the whole
  premise here is *as few decisions as possible between the thought and the
  saving of it*. Escape puts you back exactly where you were.

  ⚠ THE BOX WAS A PLAIN <textarea> UNTIL 2026-08-06, AND THAT WAS A RULE.
  14 §4 said *no rich editor here, ever*: Wispr Flow dictates into any text
  field, so voice capture worked for free as long as nothing got clever with
  the input handling. Michael reversed it, having been shown the constraint —
  the same formatting the writing sheet has is wanted here and in the pile,
  *"even though they take up a little bit of UI space"*.

  So the trade is now on the record rather than in the past: a `contenteditable`
  is still a text field and dictation still types into it, but it is no longer
  the *plainest possible* one, and if voice capture ever misbehaves this is the
  first thing to suspect. What is NOT negotiable is what the rule was protecting
  — the thought must survive the typing of it. Everything the box does to keep
  that promise (the 700ms autosave, the flush on close, ⌘/Ctrl+Enter to park
  one and start another) is unchanged.

  THE TOOLBAR SITS BELOW THE BOX, not above it. The dialog has no title and no
  instructions — *"the box says what it is by being a box with a cursor already
  in it"* — and a strip of controls above the words would be the first thing
  you meet, which is chrome introducing a box that needs no introduction.

  THE PILE IS NOT IN HERE. The room shows what you jotted; this shows a place
  to jot. Putting a recent list under the box would make the dialog a second,
  smaller version of the room — and two surfaces that both half-do the reading
  job is exactly the middle ground this workstream deleted.
*/
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import EditorToolbar from './EditorToolbar.astro';
import EntityCombo from './EntityCombo.astro';
import LinkDialog from './LinkDialog.astro';
import AltTextDialog from './AltTextDialog.astro';

interface Props {
  /**
   * Does the roster have anybody? **The fact, not the list** — see
   * `env.d.ts`'s note on `locals.hasRoster`. With nobody on it the Log tab is
   * ABSENT rather than empty (10-hq §10b), which is the same rule `notes.astro`
   * applies to its chooser row and `TaskSheet` to its goal picker.
   */
  hasRoster?: boolean;
}
const { hasRoster = false } = Astro.props;
---

{
  /* Bottom-right, above the content and below the sidebar drawer (z-30 vs the
    sidebar's z-40) so an open mobile menu covers it rather than floating over
    it. BackToTop sits at the same corner but only appears once you have
    scrolled, and it offsets itself when this is present — see app.css. */
}
<button
  id="cap-open"
  type="button"
  class="cap-fab"
  aria-label="Write something down"
  title="Write something down"
  aria-haspopup="dialog"
>
  <Icon name="ph:plus" class="text-2xl" />
</button>

<dialog id="cap-dialog" class="modal-dialog w-full max-w-xl" aria-label="Write something down">
  <div class="cap-shell font-sans">
    {
      /*
      No title on the dialog and no instructions in it. The box says what it is
      by being a box with a cursor already in it (10-hq §10i: if a sentence
      exists to justify a design decision, it belongs in the plan, not on the
      screen).

      The mount point keeps the `cap-box` id it had as a <textarea>: TipTap
      renders its own editable element inside this one, so anything looking for
      what you type wants `#cap-box [contenteditable]`.
    */
    }
    {
      /*
        ⚠ ABOVE THE BOX — WHICH REVERSES THIS FILE'S OWN RULE, and the reversal
        is the decision (plan 45 · Piece 2).

        The rule three paragraphs up says the toolbar sits BELOW the box because
        "a strip of controls above the words would be the first thing you meet,
        which is chrome introducing a box that needs no introduction." That
        argument is about FORMATTING controls, and it still holds for them. This
        row is not chrome about the box; it is the answer to *where is this
        going*, and it has to be reachable before you leave rather than after.

        It was judged on screen rather than on paper. `/lab/capture` put five
        propositions side by side — the chooser in the footer, a menu before the
        box, chips in the footer, tabs, and tabs narrowed — and Michael picked
        tabs on 2026-08-24: *"I think Kind tabs in the box is the best
        implementation thus far."*

        ⚠ AND THE TAB ONLY DECLARES. The box goes on autosaving a NOTE whichever
        tab is lit, because a half-typed task cannot be saved as a task
        (`tasks.save` wants a title, `interactions.save` wants a person uuid)
        and the whole promise here is that the thought survives the typing of
        it. The tab decides where **Done** sends it, and the destination room
        does the collecting.

        ⚠ A RADIOGROUP, NOT TABS AND NOT PRESSED BUTTONS (Piece 4, closing
        plan 45 · §9's fourth question). Piece 2 shipped this as `role="group"`
        with `aria-pressed`, deliberately refusing to imply keyboard behaviour
        it did not have. What it is, though, is an exclusive choice among five —
        which is the distinction `goal-sheet.ts` already draws beside its own
        pin: *"status is a CHOICE between four exclusive options, and the pin is
        one switch that is either on or off."* So it takes the shape the
        Observatory already has for that (plan 38 · §6.3), and `wireRadioGroups`
        gives it arrow keys and ONE tab stop instead of five — which on a box
        you reach by keyboard is the difference that matters.

        `role="tab"` stays refused: there are no tabpanels here, and the row
        does not switch what you are looking at. It says where the words go.

        ⚠ LOG CARRIES A PICKER, AND IT IS THE ONLY TAB THAT DOES (Piece 3). The
        other three declare a room and go; a log entry's room is a PERSON'S
        profile, so the person is not a field in the destination — it is the
        address of it, and there is nowhere to go until you have said who. Every
        other thing an entry needs (the kind, the date, who else was there) has
        an honest default and is collected there, which is `LogSheet`'s own
        rule: *"there is no defensible default for whose life this was."*

        ⚠ AND `When` AND `Said by` ARE NOT HERE, though plan 45 · §4c drew them.
        Piece 2 put the parse in the DESTINATION room, which took the trip they
        were saving: the task sheet reads "Friday" out of the sentence itself,
        and the quote sheet asks who said it the moment you land in it. A field
        in the corner that the next screen asks for anyway is not a shortcut,
        it is the same question twice.
      */
    }
    {
      /*
        ⚠ THE ROVING TABINDEX IS IN THE MARKUP, NOT ONLY IN THE SCRIPT, and an
        a11y spec caught the difference. `wireRadioGroups` maintains it — but it
        runs inside `boot()`, which is the LAZY half of this dialog (24 · Piece
        5), so between first paint and the composer warming, a `role="radiogroup"`
        was sitting there with five tab stops in it. Nobody could reach them
        through a closed `<dialog>`, which is exactly why it would have gone
        unnoticed: the contract the role promises was false in the DOM, and only
        a check that reads the DOM would ever say so.

        The server knows which one is checked, so it can say so. `pick()` and
        the roving tabindex take over from here.
      */
    }
    <div class="cap-tabs" role="radiogroup" aria-label="Where this is going">
      <button type="button" class="cap-tab" role="radio" data-cap-tab="jot" aria-checked="true" tabindex="0">Jot</button
      >
      <button type="button" class="cap-tab" role="radio" data-cap-tab="agenda" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1">
        Agenda
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="cap-tab" role="radio" data-cap-tab="quote" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1">
        Quote
      </button>
      <button type="button" class="cap-tab" role="radio" data-cap-tab="piece" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1">
        Piece
      </button>
      {
        hasRoster && (
          <button type="button" class="cap-tab" role="radio" data-cap-tab="log" aria-checked="false" tabindex="-1">
            Log
          </button>
        )
      }
    </div>

    <div id="cap-box" class="cap-box"></div>

    {
      /* Same toolbar as the writing sheet, images and all. `image` is on
        because a dump is often a screenshot — and files key on the fragment
        id, which "make it a piece" preserves, so a picture jotted here follows
        the thought all the way into a published essay. */
    }
    <EditorToolbar class="cap-tools" image />

    {
      /*
        ⚠ SHOWN WITH THE TAB, NOT FOLDED BEHIND A CHIP. `/lab/capture`'s narrowed
        column folded its one field on a phone to buy back pixels, and that was
        right for a field you might not want. This is not one: press Log with
        nobody named and there is no room to go to. Hiding the only question the
        tab cannot proceed without would be hiding the tab's own meaning.

        ⚠ THE OPTIONS ARRIVE LATE, ON PURPOSE. This renders empty and
        `capture.ts` fills it the first time the tab is opened — `locals.hasRoster`
        is what the layout knows, and the names are what one page view in a
        hundred actually wants (plan 45 · §4d).
      */
    }
    {
      hasRoster && (
        <div class="cap-who" data-cap-who hidden>
          <label class="f">
            <span class="f__k">
              Who <em>required</em>
            </span>
            <EntityCombo id="cap-who" name="cap-who-name" idName="cap-who-id" noun="person" placeholder="Who was it?" />
          </label>
        </div>
      )
    }

    <div class="cap-foot">
      {
        /*
        ⚠ EMPTY UNTIL THERE IS SOMETHING TRUE TO SAY, and empty again after.
        The lab shipped this with the word "Saved" already in the markup at
        opacity-0: it lied to screen readers, and it satisfied an e2e assertion
        for a save that had not happened. A whole class of bug — never pre-fill
        a status element with the success case.
      */
      }
      <span id="cap-status" class="cap-status" aria-live="polite"></span>
      <a href="/admin/notes" class="cap-link">Notes →</a>
      <button
        id="cap-new"
        type="button"
        class="btn btn-sm font-sans"
        title="Park this and start another (⌘/Ctrl + Enter)"
      >
        <Icon name="ph:plus" /> New
      </button>
      <button id="cap-done" type="button" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm font-sans">Done</button>
    </div>
  </div>
</dialog>

{
  /* Outside the capture dialog, not inside it: a <dialog> opened from within
    another <dialog> stacks in the top layer above its opener, which is what we
    want, but nesting the element would trap focus in the wrong one. Ids
    because the pile mounts its own pair on the same page. */
}
<LinkDialog id="cap-link-dialog" />
<AltTextDialog id="cap-alt-dialog" />

<script>
  import '../../scripts/capture';
</script>
